# text = There are some (many?) in STS that propose a moderate stance-- who do not buy into the extreme reletavism of some in the strong program nor naive empircism/realism, who believe that scientific concepts are unstable and dynamic but nevertheless similar and worth study, who think of scientists as (for the most part) good people (like the rest of us) but who are not beyond forming unethical powerful cartels. I agree with Lieberman, that scientists do many good things, but if G&L are suggesting that their work is inherently "good" then "the job" of STS is first to show the contrary.
